/-! # Cohomological Brown representability on the constructible localizing hull

Source: blueprint `sk-thm-existence` (3), in its well-generated form.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-adic-localizing-cohomological-representability)
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits
open CategoryTheory.Pretriangulated.Opposite
open scoped CategoryTheory.Obj CategoryTheory.Functor

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.ProEtale.CondensedRing

open Scheme

variable (Λ : Sheaf (coherentTopology Profinite.{u}) Ab.{u + 1})
  [MonObj Λ] [IsCommMonObj Λ] (ℓ : ℕ) [Fact ℓ.Prime]
  {k : Type u} [Field k] [Fact (ringChar k).Prime] [Fact (ringChar k ≠ ℓ)]
  (X : Over (Spec (.of k)))
  [LocallyOfFiniteType X.hom] [QuasiCompact X.hom] [IsSeparated X.hom]
  (hΛ : IsEllAdicCoefficient ℓ Λ inferInstance)

include ℓ k hΛ

/-- Brown representability on the localizing hull of adic constructible complexes.

Source: blueprint `sk-thm-existence` (3), `det-existence-localizing.proof.md` (3);
Neeman, *On the derived category of sheaves on a manifold*, Theorem 0.2;
Krause, *Localization theory for triangulated categories*, Theorems 7.2.1 and 5.1.1.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-adic-localizing-cohomological-representability)

Proof: the ambient category of module objects is Grothendieck abelian, so its
unbounded derived category is well generated. Bounded constructible complexes form
an essentially small category. Indeed, fix a small model of the site. Covers and
finite presentation matrices on their members form a small collection of data; the
modules they present are fixed up to isomorphism. Descent is specified by isomorphisms
on pairwise overlaps, satisfying the equality on triple overlaps. These choices form
a set because the module category is locally small. A finite stratification contributes
only small data as well. Glue across an open stratum and its closed complement using
the localization exact sequence; extensions of two fixed sheaves are classified by
their degree-one derived Hom set. Induct over the finitely many strata. This gives a
small set of constructible sheaves, without assuming the presentation entries constant.
For a fixed cohomological interval, induct on its length using
truncation triangles: each next extension is classified by an ambient Hom set. The
union over the integer endpoints is still small (`det-brown-hypotheses-small`).
Choose a small skeleton S. The displayed hull is exactly the localizing subcategory
generated by S, by its intersection universal property. Its proved countable-coproduct
idempotent splitting and retract-closure instances also give the thick-subcategory
convention for localizing subcategories used by Krause. A localizing subcategory
generated by a set in a well-generated category is well generated (Krause 7.2.1).
In detail choose an uncountable regular cardinal for which the ambient category is well generated
and every object of S is alpha-compact; the alpha-localizing closure of S is a small
family of alpha-small perfect generators of the hull. Perfect-generator Brown
representability (Krause 5.1.1) now represents H because H is cohomological and carries
coproducts to products. This argument needs no compactness of S or cohomological
bound on the base field. Small means universe u+1 throughout, including the skeleton,
Hom sets and values of H, so no larger indexing universe is required.
-/
theorem ellAdic_localizing_cohomological_isRepresentable
    (H : (boundedConstructibleLocalizingClosure Λ X.left).FullSubcategoryᵒᵖ ⥤
      AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}) [H.IsHomological]
    (hH : ∀ I : Type (u + 1), PreservesLimitsOfShape (Discrete I) H) :
    (H ⋙ forget AddCommGrpCat).IsRepresentable := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.ProEtale.CondensedRing
